From bdf8fbbe8b834d3d9126d5a6173fb32c62d25090 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:05:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers, windowing/gbm, skin.estuary: the GPU time of
 each pass in the log and on the Dolby Vision page

To measure on a GPU what each part of a native Dolby Vision frame costs,
advancedsettings.xml can turn on the timing of every pass
(dvbridge_render_time_passes()): <video><dvpasstimes>true</dvpasstimes>
</video>. It is read when the bridge is first initialized, which makes
libplacebo with its timers on OpenGL ES (gles_timer_queries,
libplacebo-9944), and kodi.log says whether libplacebo's passes and the
bridge's own are timed (GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query). Off by default:
each pass then begins and ends a timer query.

With it:
- the 10-s line of native output ends with the six passes that took the
  most GPU time in the window: "; GPU time by pass, the most first
  (average / longest ms, times): "<libplacebo's description of the pass,
  or the bridge's name of it>" a / m xN; ...";
- the Dolby Vision page of the player information has a row more, "GPU
  by pass", under the GPU time of a frame: the passes that took the most,
  by short names (CDVPassTimes::Short(): the plane of the composer,
  "output" for libplacebo's pass that draws into the target, "vertical
  scaling", what a pass of the bridge does), in at most 36 characters,
  which with the label fit the 885 pixels of a row of the page in
  Estuary's font14 in English and Spanish; the panel of the page is a row
  taller only then;
- a window in which the GL says it lost the times of its queries
  (GL_GPU_DISJOINT_EXT, which the timer of the frame reads every frame)
  has the times of its passes dropped as well.
Without it nothing of this is made, and the page and the log are as they
were.

Not seen on a screen (Kodi's GUI does not start on the software GL); the
text and the arithmetic have a host test, and the page a check of its rows
against the panel and of their width in the font.
